  abstract = {Recent economic troubles in the US and abroad highlight the importance\textbackslash nof family financial capability, including an understanding of financial\textbackslash nmarkets. Financial capability is the foundation for desired financial\textbackslash nbehaviours, such as saving, budgeting, using credit wisely and planning.\textbackslash nStudy participants, a subsample of respondents to a Turkish university\textbackslash nfinancial literacy survey (n=374), who reported uninterrupted income for\textbackslash na 3-year period were grouped as planners' and non-planners'. These\textbackslash ngroupings allowed examination of the relationships between planning,\textbackslash nfinancial management decisions, and differential outcomes in daily\textbackslash nhousehold financial well-being. The practice of preferred financial\textbackslash nmanagement behaviours was predictive of debt. This research makes a\textbackslash nunique contribution to the literature, demonstrating the importance of\textbackslash nuninterrupted income over income amount in support of the planning\textbackslash nprocess. Findings of this study have implications for professionals in\textbackslash nthe family and consumer sciences field and other practitioners assisting\textbackslash nconsumers with improving financial management outcomes.},
